n to ensure that the vaccine is safe  many people just are inherently  distrustful of vaccines and because of  this  going so fast and people not really  understanding why it was able to go so  fast there  has been a lot of concern about maybe  it's going too fast and it's not safe  but  i hope that i explain to you some of the  basic  biology and scientific underpinnings of  why it was able to go faster this time  than it has ever gone before  some of the pre-established  relationships and  information we had and uh  and that we have generated a lot of data  both in animal models  and in humans and have gone through all  the typical phases but it's just all  done  in parallel and at the same time a lot  of  risk is being taken on advanced  manufacturing and  process development for these different  products that typically  would be done serially instead of in  parallel so a lot of money  has been put at risk but i hope i've  been able to  convince you that none of the essential  steps in  safety have been skipped and in fact  in some ways they've been scrutinized  even more heavily  because of this speed than we  often do thank you so much  dr graham we have time for one more  question i want to give you some closing  remarks time as well  when will you know the answer from the  phase three study  these are endpoint driven trials so  the second doses in at least the moderna  trial started  a few weeks ago and so 14 days after  that second dose was  september 7th so we've only been  counting new  cases symptomatic cases that are pcr  confirmed  starting from the 7th of september and  the first interim analysis will be when  we get up to 53 cases  the next analysis we think that could  give an answer would be at 151 cases so  it really is a matter of  infection rates and where enrollment has  happened and who gets infected and who  isn't  and potentially how effective the  vaccine is if it's highly effective  versus modestly effective it would  affect the timing  so 